{"author": "math_harness_cgm", "claim": "The left-hand side is the sum of the first n odd numbers: LHS(n) = sum_{k=1}^n (2k - 1), with the empty-sum convention LHS(0) = 0.", "evidence": "Direct rewriting of the displayed sum 1 + 3 + ... + (2n-1); the k-th term 2k-1 for k = 1..n ranges exactly over the first n odd naturals.", "fact_id": null, "glossary": {}, "id": "2a96a5450d8b1927", "kind": "conclusion", "links": {"predecessors": [], "subgoal": "goal:root"}, "status": "unverified", "timestamp_utc": "2026-09-27T12:03:03.747904Z", "verifiable": true}
{"author": "math_harness_cgm", "claim": "Each odd number is a difference of consecutive squares: 2k - 1 = k^2 - (k-1)^2.", "evidence": "Pure algebra: k^2 - (k^2 - 2k + 1) = 2k - 1.", "fact_id": null, "glossary": {}, "id": "7367ffd670e3e62d", "kind": "conclusion", "links": {"predecessors": [], "subgoal": "goal:root"}, "status": "unverified", "timestamp_utc": "2026-09-27T12:03:03.747904Z", "verifiable": true}
{"author": "math_harness_cgm", "claim": "Consequently, sum_{k=1}^n (2k-1) telescopes: sum_{k=1}^n (k^2 - (k-1)^2) = n^2 - 0^2 = n^2.", "evidence": "Telescoping sums: sum_{k=1}^n (a_k - a_{k-1}) = a_n - a_0 with a_k = k^2; this is itself a one-line induction or a term-by-term cancellation argument.", "fact_id": null, "glossary": {}, "id": "69adc0ac6c28d7bf", "kind": "conclusion", "links": {"predecessors": [], "subgoal": "goal:root"}, "status": "unverified", "timestamp_utc": "2026-09-27T12:03:03.747904Z", "verifiable": true}
{"author": "math_harness_cgm", "claim": "The claim also follows from the closed-form formula for arithmetic series: sum_{k=1}^n (2k-1) = n*(1 + (2n-1))/2 = n^2.", "evidence": "Arithmetic series formula sum of n terms with first term a1 and last term an equals n(a1+an)/2; plugging a1 = 1, an = 2n-1 gives n*(2n)/2 = n^2. The formula itself requires proof (pairing or induction).\n\nFragile: The arithmetic-series formula must be proved, not cited as known; workers must discharge it within the plan.", "fact_id": null, "glossary": {}, "id": "68959cf9c9fdc7d5", "kind": "conclusion", "links": {"predecessors": [], "subgoal": "goal:root"}, "status": "unverified", "timestamp_utc": "2026-09-27T12:03:03.747904Z", "verifiable": true}
{"author": "math_harness_cgm", "claim": "Necessary-condition check on small cases: n = 0 gives 0 = 0; n = 1 gives 1 = 1; n = 2 gives 4 = 4; n = 3 gives 9 = 9. Statement is true and very likely provable by elementary means.", "evidence": "Direct computation.", "fact_id": null, "glossary": {}, "id": "108b45028ce80fb8", "kind": "conclusion", "links": {"predecessors": [], "subgoal": "goal:root"}, "status": "unverified", "timestamp_utc": "2026-09-27T12:03:03.747904Z", "verifiable": true}
{"author": "math_harness_cgm", "claim": "The inductive step reduces to (n+1)^2 - n^2 = 2n+1, i.e., the increment of the sum matches the increment of the square.", "evidence": "If sum_{k=1}^n (2k-1) = n^2 then sum_{k=1}^{n+1} (2k-1) = n^2 + (2n+1) = (n+1)^2; conversely the identity n^2 + (2n+1) = (n+1)^2 is plain algebra.", "fact_id": null, "glossary": {}, "id": "3188572e6ab42c4b", "kind": "conclusion", "links": {"predecessors": [], "subgoal": "goal:root"}, "status": "unverified", "timestamp_utc": "2026-09-27T12:03:03.747904Z", "verifiable": true}

